ike a Karņikāra bough in a forest, with all its blossoms shrivelled up. The sons of this exalted lady are the youthful and heroic Lakşmaņa and ſatrughna, having truth for their prowess, and resembling celestials in shape.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.761,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-92-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2086022","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"941c3f7597abba5561ce70baa3eab19617e9edadcd5b996290d18adc01cb7e44","compact":"10295cbddf5ec40bb94179277f22c6f71d90f2cb25e80df4238c67bb4020aa03","nasal":"722eb075dd2cdd9ae5a97aac8aaaf30ea3c1d1d9fb0b8f4e2f1db685655ae99e"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-86-23","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":86,"verse":23,"english":"She that stands at her left hand, in dejected guise, is the noble Sumitrā afflicted with sorrow, the second wife of the monarch, like a Karņikāra bough in a forest, with all its blossoms shrivelled up. The sons of this exalted lady are the youthful and heroic Lakşmaņa and ſatrughna, having truth for their prowess, and resembling celestials in shape.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-92-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2086023","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"2d49bf47c043b8ef854f83a23b7ae2422b52b86e914ce52accc68bb91883d62c","compact":"291154ae7d57ee70f0ad4a6649e862aeea72b78f88c4f51db8eca1de1664fe12","nasal":"291154ae7d57ee70f0ad4a6649e862aeea72b78f88c4f51db8eca1de1664fe12"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-86-24","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":86,"verse":24,"english":"And her in consequence of whose act those foremost of men have come by crushing misfortune, and the king Dasaratha has gone to heaven, deprived of his son, wrathful and proud of her good fortune, setting her heart of wealth, Kaikeyi, dishonourable, although endowed with the semblance of honour, do you know this wicked one intent on sin as my mother. In her do I perceive the root of my mighty misfortune.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-92-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2086024","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5eb81702bac982a565fc23edf20bf1aa3ee07153669f0216bd397664cadf39e8","compact":"b5317831307a66177cf2b56117fec1f20fcc32eff76b3b8d53129064793fef1a","nasal":"b5317831307a66177cf2b56117fec1f20fcc32eff76b3b8d53129064793fef1a"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-86-25","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":86,"verse":25,"english":"And her in consequence of whose act those foremost of men have come by crushing misfortune, and the king Dasaratha has gone to heaven, deprived of his son, wrathful and proud of her good fortune, setting her heart of wealth, Kaikeyi, dishonourable, although endowed with the semblance of honour, do you know this wicked one intent on sin as my mother. In her do I perceive the root of my mighty misfortune.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.908,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-92-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2086025","sanskritFile":"Ram02.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"5a84dd31dc00c25d309318991db1413d69b11afd481409430f4c36c4e344466f","compact":"56d075e83266a5f358292d2e74b3960ac1056a3a4e02d6bca2b58608a6980906","nasal":"66bbc11943dfb6c244c0fc8f2578f9a5bac255a59fc8265a0c467fbb973a8fbb"}},{"id":"VRCE-2-86-26","kanda":2,"kandaId":"ayodhya","sarga":86,"verse":26,"english":"Having said this, with his words choked with emotion, that best of men with his eyes reddened, sighed like an enraged serpent.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.574,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-2-92-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"2086026","sanskritFile":"R